        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Lepanto</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">7 October 1571</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Gulf of Patras, off the Greek coast near Lepanto (ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a>, Turkish İnebahtı), in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a>: 206 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and six galleasses, with about 30,000 fighting troops and 40,000 sailors and oarsmen. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> under Ali Pasha: 211 galleys and 63 galliots, with about 37,000 to 41,000 oarsmen, virtually all of them slaves, and up to 34,000 soldiers.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> captured 117 Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and sank or destroyed some 83 others. Losses were about 7,500 dead for the Christians and about 30,000 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, though other sources give 10,000 and 40,000.</span></div>
