    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Lepanto was fought between rowing ships, the last major engagement in the Western world of that kind, and was in essence an infantry battle on floating platforms.</li><li>Both fleets depended on oarsmen who were largely enslaved, convicts or prisoners, and some of those freed from Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> took up arms during the fighting.</li><li>The victory won no territory back: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> accepted peace in March 1573 and ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> rebuilt their fleet within months.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Ali Pasha is supposed to have promised his Christian galley slaves their liberty if he won, while on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> side the galley slaves were freed from their chains before the fighting began.</p></aside>
    </section>

