    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1570</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> and other Venetian possessions on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> fall to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1571</span><span class="rf-text">Famagusta surrenders on 1 August, and the pope's blessed banner reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 14 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1571</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> fleet leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> on 15 and 16 September, then learns that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> have moved to Lepanto</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1571</span><span class="rf-text">On 7 October the fleets meet in the Gulf of Patras and the fighting begins around noon</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1572</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> dies and the League begins to unravel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1572</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uluj-ali">Occhiali</a> sails with 200 newly built ships and skirmishes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a>'s fleet</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1573</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> makes peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> on 7 March, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-venetian-war-1570-1573">War of Cyprus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1574</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v.jpg" alt="Pope Pius V" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bartolomeo_Passarotti_-_Pius_V.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bartolomeo Passarotti and workshop, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> was promoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> to rescue Famagusta, the Venetian colony on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, besieged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in early 1571 after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> and other Venetian possessions in 1570. On 1 August the Venetians surrendered after being assured that they could leave Cyprus freely. The Ottoman commander Lala Kara Mustafa Pasha, who had lost some 50,000 men in the siege, broke his word, imprisoned the Venetians and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-antonio-bragadin">Marco Antonio Bragadin</a> flayed alive.</p><p>The League's members were the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> with its Italian kingdoms, the Papal States, the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, the Duchies of Savoy, Urbino and Tuscany, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> and others. All of them saw the Ottoman navy as a threat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade and to continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was the largest financial contributor, though the Spaniards would have preferred to keep most of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> for their own wars against the sultanates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary Coast</a>.</p><p>The fleet was placed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a>, half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcantonio-colonna">Marcantonio Colonna</a> as his principal deputy. The contingents met the Venetian force under Sebastiano Venier at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> in July and August 1571. On the Ottoman side, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> had issued express orders to destroy the Christian fleet.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/galley"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galley.jpg" alt="Galley" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/galley">Galley</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sbonski_de_Passabon-Galere_a_la_voille.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Henri Sbonski de Passebon, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> had 206 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and six galleasses, large new galleys with masts, sails and heavy artillery that the Venetians had developed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> supplied 109 galleys and all six galleasses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and its territories 49 galleys, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> 27 and the Papal States seven. The fleet carried about 30,000 fighting troops, among them 7,000 Spanish infantry, 7,000 German landsknechts, 6,000 Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in Spanish pay and 5,000 Venetian soldiers, and was manned by 40,000 sailors and oarsmen. The historian George Finlay estimated that over 25,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> fought on the League's side, as soldiers and as sailors or oarsmen.</p><p>The Ottoman fleet under Ali Pasha, with the corsairs Mehmed Sirocco and Uluç Ali, had 211 galleys, 63 galliots and smaller vessels. Its oarsmen, about 37,000 to 41,000, were virtually all slaves, many of them Christians captured earlier. It had 13,000 experienced sailors and 25,000 soldiers, though the number of soldiers may have been up to 34,000, and was short of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>. Christians are estimated to have had 1,815 guns and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> 750, with insufficient ammunition. The Ottomans relied on composite bowmen, the League on arquebusiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a> mixed the nationalities in each squadron, and sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> in formation. Divisions of 53, 62 and 53 galleys stood from north to south, with a reserve of 38 under Álvaro de Bazán. The Ottomans stood in three divisions under Sirocco, Ali Pasha and Uluç Ali.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Lepanto&#8221; cites where it covers Deployment and order of battle, Holy League deployment, Turkish deployment, Comparison:</p><ul><li>Angus Konstam, Lepanto 1571: The Greatest Naval Battle of the Renaissance (Osprey Publishing, 2003)</li><li>John F. Guilmartin, Gunpowder &amp; Galleys: Changing Technology &amp; Mediterranean Warfare at Sea in the 16th Century (Cambridge University Press, 1974)</li><li>Kenneth M. Setton, The Papacy and the Levant (1204–1571), Vol. IV: The Sixteenth Century (Memoirs of the American Philosophical Society, 1984)</li><li>David Brewer, Greece, the Hidden Centuries: Turkish Rule from the Fall of Constantinople to Greek Independence (Bloomsbury Publishing, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-of-austria.jpg" alt="John of Austria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Don_Juan_d%27Austria_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The wind was at first against the League, but around noon it shifted in its favour. Galleasses stationed in front of its line opened fire at close quarters on the foremost Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and confused their order. First contact came around noon between the squadrons of Agostino Barbarigo and Sirocco near the northern shore. Sirocco, who knew the depth of the water, got galleys between Barbarigo and the coast. The ships came so close that they formed an almost continuous platform of hand-to-hand fighting. Barbarigo was killed by an arrow to the eye, but Bazán arrived with the reserve, and Sirocco was killed and beheaded. Christian galley slaves freed from Ottoman ships were given arms and joined in.</p><p>In the centre Ali Pasha's galley drove into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a>'s Real as far as the fourth rowing bench, and Spanish infantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a> fought around the two flagships. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> came alongside and helped to push the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> off the Real. The Ottoman flagship was boarded and its entire crew killed, Ali Pasha among them. After two hours the Ottomans were beaten on the left and in the centre, though fighting went on for two more.</p><p>On the League's right, Gianandrea Doria sailed south instead of taking his station. He later said he was trying to prevent an enveloping move, but his captains took his signals for treachery. Uluç Ali then fell on the gap and on the flagship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights of Malta</a>, until Bazán's reserve again came up and he retreated with the Knights' flag. Many Greek rowers on Ottoman galleys are said to have mutinied and delivered their ships to the League.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Lepanto&#8221; cites where it covers Deployment and order of battle, Comparison, Battle:</p><ul><li>Angus Konstam, Lepanto 1571: The Greatest Naval Battle of the Renaissance (Osprey Publishing, 2003)</li><li>Geoffrey Parker, The Military Revolution: Military Innovation and the Rise of the West, 1500–1800 (Cambridge University Press, 1996)</li><li>Cathal J. Nolan, The Age of Wars of Religion, 1000-1650 [2 volumes]: An Encyclopedia of Global Warfare and Civilization (Greenwood Press, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-venetian-war-1570-1573"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottoman-venetian-war-1570-1573.jpg" alt="Ottoman–Venetian War (1570–1573)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-venetian-war-1570-1573">Ottoman–Venetian War (1570–1573)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Lepanto_1571.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the end of the battle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> had captured 117 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and sunk or destroyed some 83 others. Around ten thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> were taken prisoner, and many thousands of Christian slaves were rescued. The League lost around 7,500 dead and the Ottomans about 30,000, while other sources put the figures higher, at 10,000 Christians and 40,000 Ottomans. Celebrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> included triumphs and pageants with Turkish slaves in chains.</p><p>The Ottomans rebuilt fast. About six months later they had built more than 150 galleys, eight galleasses and 250 ships in all, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uluj-ali">Occhiali</a> sailed with 200 of them in July 1572. The League did not take advantage of its victory. After the death of Pius V on 1 May 1572 its diverging interests showed, an attack on Navarino was abandoned, and in 1573 the fleet did not sail, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a> taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> instead. Venice, fearing for its Dalmatian possessions and eager to resume trade, negotiated alone. The peace of 7 March 1573 disbanded the League and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-venetian-war-1570-1573">War of Cyprus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> was ceded to the Ottomans, Venice agreed to pay 300,000 ducats, and the Ottomans occupied fertile parts of the hinterland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>.</p><p>In 1574 the Ottomans retook Tunis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokollu-mehmed-pasha">Sokollu Mehmed Pasha</a> is quoted as telling a Venetian envoy that in winning Cyprus the Ottomans had cut off an arm, while in defeating their fleet the League had only shaved a beard.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Lepanto&#8221; cites where it covers Following operations, Consequences:</p><ul><li>Roger Charles Anderson, Naval wars in the Levant, 1559-1853 (Princeton University Press, 1952)</li><li>Caroline Finkel, Osman's Dream: The Story of the Ottoman Empire 1300–1923 (John Murray, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first comprehensive account of the war was Giovanni Pietro Contarini's History of the Events, published in 1572, a few months after the battle, and the only full historical account by an immediate commentator. It praises the victory, but also examines its meaning and the political philosophy of conflict in the early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Venetian and Spanish prints, poems and paintings of 1571 and after, among them 233 Italian poems printed between 1571 and 1573, show how the Christian side saw the battle.</p><p>The figures vary. The strength of the Ottoman soldiers is given as 25,000 and as perhaps up to 34,000, and the losses as 7,500 and 30,000 or as 10,000 and 40,000. The speeches attributed to the commanders are given with hedges: Ali Pasha is supposed to have promised the galley slaves their liberty, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>' throwing of oranges and lemons is something that is said. Doria's conduct on the right is also a matter of interpretation, since he gave one explanation and his captains another.</p><p>Historians also differ over the weight of the battle. Paul K. Davis calls it more a moral than a military victory. Others have compared it to Salamis. It was often named as the turning point of Ottoman expansion, though this was by no means an immediate consequence, because the League did not recover any lost territory and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> rebuilt, reasserting supremacy in the eastern Mediterranean.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/titian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/titian.jpg" alt="Titian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titian_-_Self-portrait_(Museo_del_Prado).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The League credited the victory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, whose help it had asked for through the Rosary. Pius V instituted the feast of Our Lady of Victory, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> now celebrates as the feast of Our Lady of the Rosary. Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Nafpaktos</a> credit the victory to the Panagia Nafpaktiotissa. Philip II used it to strengthen his position as defender of Christendom, and it was of great symbolic importance while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was torn by wars of religion.</p><p>The battle confirmed the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> into an eastern half under firm Ottoman control and a western half under the Spanish Crown and its Italian allies, and it halted Ottoman encroachment on Italian territories. Yet the loss of many experienced crews made Ottoman maritime ventures harder after 1580. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a> would displace the galley, which Lepanto was the last major engagement to depend on, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs went on raiding western coasts until the 19th century.</p><p>Paintings by Veronese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> and in the Doge's Palace, the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>, motets, and epics by Juan Latino and others commemorate it, as does the poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a>, written about 1585. G. K. Chesterton's poem of 1911 connects the battle with Cervantes, who also fought in it. In 1942 Elizabeth Goudge used the battle in a war-time novel to call up British resistance to Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Lepanto on 7 October 1571 a fleet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, defeated the Ottoman fleet in the Gulf of Patras. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">Galley</a> slaves served on both sides. The League did not follow up the victory, and by the peace of 1573 Venice ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</em></p>

